 [rel('cdn_working_class','Uses feminist critiques to reconsider gender, race and ethnicity within accounts of working-class formation.',['wcreq_sangster_2000'],'Reuse of the recorded abstract and bounded 2000 essay readings. The field’s masculinist starting points are Sangster’s diagnosis. This does not infer a single causal genealogy from political organizing.'),rel('cdn_women_gender','Questions treating women’s history and gender history as opposed stages in an inevitable methodological succession.',['cdnsrc_sangster_1995','cdnsrc_sangster_1996','cdnsrc_sangster_2011'],'The 2011 introduction’s own retrospect supports this formulation; existing addenda separately record full readings of the two original essays. The disagreement and Sangster’s concessions remain situated, not an assertion that all gender historians held one position.')],
